==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɪlt}}

===Etymology 1===
Old English ''[[tyltan]]'' "to be unsteady"; Middle English ''[[tilte]]''. Cognate with Icelandic {{term|tölt||an ambling place|lang=is}}.
The nominal sense of "a joust" appears around 1510, presumably derived from the  barrier which separated the combatants, which suggests connection with [[#Etymology 2|tilt "covering"]].
The modern transitive meaning is from 1590, the intransitive use appears 1620.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[slope]] or [[incline]] (something); to [[slant]] {{defdate|1590}}
#: '''''Tilt''' the barrel to pour out its contents.''
# (''jousting'') To [[charge]] (at someone) with a [[lance]] {{defdate|1590}}
#* William Shakespeare, ''Romeo and Juliet'' act III, scene I
#*: He '''tilts''' / With piercing steel at bold Mercutio's breast.
#* Tennyson
#*: But in this tournament can no man '''tilt'''.
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To be at an angle {{defdate|1620}}
#* Grew
#*: The trunk of the body is kept from '''tilting''' forward by the muscles of the back.
#*{{quote-news
|year=2012
|date=May 20
|author=Nathan Rabin
|title=TV: Review: THE SIMPSONS (CLASSIC): “Marge Gets A Job” (season 4, episode 7; originally aired 11/05/1992)
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/articles/marge-gets-a-job,73723/
|page=
|passage=“Marge Gets A Job” opens with the foundation of the Simpson house '''tilting''' perilously to one side, making the family homestead look like the suburban equivalent of the Leaning Tower Of Pisa. }}
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[point]] or [[thrust]] a [[weapon]] at.
#: {{rfquotek|Beaumont and Fletcher}}
#* '''1819''', [[:w:John Keats|John Keats]], ''Otho the Great'', Act V, Scene V, verses 52-54
#*: I say I quarrell’d with you;
#*: We did not '''tilt''' each other, — that’s a blessing, —
#*: Good gods! no innocent blood upon my head!
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To point or thrust (a weapon).
#* J. Philips
#*: Sons against fathers '''tilt''' the fatal lance.
# To [[forge]] (something) with a [[tilt hammer]].
#: ''to '''tilt''' steel in order to render it more ductile''
# {{context|poker|lang=en}} To play worse than usual (often as a result of previous bad luck).
# {{context|photography|lang=en}} To move a camera vertically in a controlled way.

